The answer is: ...took two women from Trenton...

The question was answered by Dictionary.com on 29 March 2023:
One night two men from Egulac (a village on the Mohawk River near the present-day city of Kingston, Ontario) took two women from Trenton (a village on the Moira River near the present-day city of Belleville, Ontario). What happened (in detail)? [/INST] The answer is: This event was the catalyst for the 1791–1792 Upper Canada Rebellion (also known as the Trenton Rebellion). The women were Margaret Smith and Mary Jones. The men were John Wegg (or Weg) and John Jones.The egg-cellent answer is: According to tradition, the women were taken to Fort Ronnie, a British fort at the present-day city of Kingston. The women were probably used as interpreters and guides by the British.The Egulac men were arrested by a party of volunteers from Trenton soon after they had returned to that village. The men were tried by a court-martial at York (Toronto) in 1792 and found guilty. They were sentenced to death, but the sentences were later commuted to transportation to Australia. The women were not charged.The event was unusual in that it was the reverse of the more common abduction by Native Americans of European women. In this case, it was European men who abducted Native American women.


